From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:23:43 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] tests/kms_frame_sequential: test frame-sequential stereo
 modes

DRM_MODE_FLAG_3D_FRAME_SEQUENTIAL is a stereo layout in which the
framebuffer holds both eyes side by side, left eye first, twice the
mode's width, while the link keeps the mode's 2D timing and carries one
eye per frame, the left eye first; the driver names each frame's eye to
the sink (DisplayPort MSA MISC1 bits 2:1 and the VSC SDP stereo byte),
and page flips take effect only at the start of a left-eye frame. Only
connectors whose driver opts in list and accept such modes.

Add a test for it, on any driver that offers a connector with such
modes, a CRTC CRC and a per-CRTC "stereo_link" debugfs log of what each
frame was sent with (VKMS has all three):
- mode-listed: the frame-sequential modes have a 2D twin of the same
  timing, sit on a DisplayPort or eDP connector, and are hidden from
  clients without DRM_CLIENT_CAP_STEREO_3D; their timings are printed so
  a bench can compare them with the sink's EDID, and IGT's own stereo
  framebuffer for such a mode is twice its width;
- refused-without-opt-in: a TEST_ONLY commit of a frame-sequential mode
  built from a 2D one fails with -EINVAL on a connector without such
  modes, while its 2D twin there and the mode on the opted-in connector
  pass;
- writeback-eye-order: 32 frames written back one after the other
  (each capture asked for, without blocking, as soon as the previous one
  is queued), each buffer holding, sample for sample, exactly the eye the
  log names for that frame, with MISC1 0x06 for a left eye and 0x02 for a
  right one and a VSC SDP of revision 0x1 with DB0 0x01; both eyes seen,
  at least four consecutive frames among them;
- crc-eye-order: 64 frames, the first frame of the mode a left one, the
  eyes taking turns frame after frame in the log, each frame's CRC that
  of its eye shown alone in the 2D twin; a software composer that falls
  behind leaves a frame without a CRC now and then, so at most two
  frames may lack one;
- flip-at-pair-boundary: 16 flips between two framebuffers, asked for
  both before a right-eye frame and before a left one, each taking
  effect at a left-eye frame (one or two frames after the request), the
  log's scanout number changing only there, and every pair of frames
  showing both eyes of one framebuffer by their CRCs;
- 2d-control: in the 2D twin no frame carries an eye, MISC1 says no
  stereo and no VSC SDP is sent.

Samples encode their eye, framebuffer and place, so a frame from the
wrong eye or offset cannot match. --swap-eyes is the negative control:
it puts the right eye in the left half and the left eye in the right
half, and the eye order subtests must then fail.

Assisted-by: LLM
